{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n -i \"live display|update.*display|transient|refresh_per_second|overwrite|cursor\" docs/source/live.rst docs/source/console.rst docs/source/reference/console.rst'","aggregated_output":"docs/source/live.rst:3:Live Display\ndocs/source/live.rst:6:Progress bars and status indicators use a *live* display to animate parts of the terminal. You can build custom live displays with the :class:`~rich.live.Live` class.\ndocs/source/live.rst:8:For a demonstration of a live display, run the following command::\ndocs/source/live.rst:19:To create a live display, construct a :class:`~rich.live.Live` object with a renderable and use it as a context manager. The live display will persist for the duration of the context. You can update the renderable to update the display::\ndocs/source/live.rst:32:    with Live(table, refresh_per_second=4):  # update 4 times a second to feel fluid\ndocs/source/live.rst:42:You can also change the renderable on-the-fly by calling the :meth:`~rich.live.Live.update` method. This may be useful if the information you wish to display is too dynamic to generate by updating a single renderable. Here is an example::\ndocs/source/live.rst:66:    with Live(generate_table(), refresh_per_second=4) as live:\ndocs/source/live.rst:75:You can opt to show a Live display in the \"alternate screen\" by setting ``screen=True`` on the constructor. This will allow your live display to go full screen and restore the command prompt on exit.\ndocs/source/live.rst:79:Transient display\ndocs/source/live.rst:82:Normally when you exit live context manager (or call :meth:`~rich.live.Live.stop`) the last refreshed item remains in the terminal with the cursor on the following line.\ndocs/source/live.rst:83:You can also make the live display disappear on exit by setting ``transient=True`` on the Live constructor.\ndocs/source/live.rst:88:By default, the live display will refresh 4 times a second. You can set the refresh rate with the ``refresh_per_second`` argument on the :class:`~rich.live.Live` constructor.\ndocs/source/live.rst:97:By default, the live display will display ellipsis if the renderable is too large for the terminal. You can adjust this by setting the\ndocs/source/live.rst:106:    Once the live display stops on a non-transient renderable, the last frame will render as **visible** since it doesn't have to be cleared.\ndocs/source/live.rst:112:The Live class will create an internal Console object which you can access via ``live.console``. If you print or log to this console, the output will be displayed *above* the live display. Here's an example::\ndocs/source/live.rst:124:    with Live(table, refresh_per_second=4) as live:  # update 4 times a second to feel fluid\ndocs/source/live.rst:141:    If you are passing in a file console, the live display only show the last item once the live context is left.\ndocs/source/live.rst:146:To avoid breaking the live display visuals, Rich will redirect ``stdout`` and ``stderr`` so that you can use the builtin ``print`` statement.\ndocs/source/live.rst:162:for deeper examples of live displaying.\ndocs/source/console.rst:421:Setting the environment variable ``TERM`` to ``\"dumb\"`` or ``\"unknown\"`` will disable color/style and some features that require moving the cursor, such as progress bars.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
